(window.DVR_PAPERS=window.DVR_PAPERS||{})["ec-easy-2026-009"]={"v":1,"id":"ec-easy-2026-009","title":"ESE 2026 DEMO TEST EC","sourceTitle":"ESE 2026 DEMO TEST EC","branch":"ELECTRONICS ENGINEERING","code":"EC","provider":"DVRUO EASY","providerKey":"easy","series":"GATE 2026 Online Test Series - Electronics Engineering","seriesId":"ec-easy-2026","num":9,"category":"demo","year":"2026","format":"A","durationSec":10800,"info":{"meta":"Package: GATE 2026 + GATE 2025 Online Test Series - Electronics Engineering • Questions: 150","package":"GATE 2026 + GATE 2025 Online Test Series - Electronics Engineering"},"source":"ELECTRONICS ENGINEERING/MADE EASY/GATE_2026_+_GATE_2025_Online_Test_Series_Electronics_Engineering/017_ESE 2026 DEMO TEST EC.html","questions":[{"n":1,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • ELECTRONICS ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>Consider cache memory with 70% hit ratio and 60 ns access time. The main memory access time is 450 ns. What is the average access time of CPU?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"195 ns"},{"key":"B","html":"200 ns"},{"key":"C","html":"223 ns"},{"key":"D","html":"215 ns"}],"answer":["A"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(a)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/46/4673264f3d38f2bf404b.png\" /></div>"},{"n":2,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • ELECTRONICS ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>Which of the following statements is/are correct?<br />\n1. IO devices are electromagnetic components and CPU is a electronic component.<br />\n2. IO interface chip is responsible for IO operations.<br />\n3. Main memory space is synchronised to program size using cache memory.<br />\n4. Main memory speed is synchronised to CPU speed using virtual memory.</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"1, 2 and 3"},{"key":"B","html":"1 and 2 only"},{"key":"C","html":"2, 3 and 4"},{"key":"D","html":"1, 3 and 4"}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(b)<br />\n&bull; Peripherals are electromechnical and electromagnetic devices and CPU and memory are<br />\nelectronic devices. Therefore, a conversion of signal values may be needed which is done by<br />\nIO interface.<br />\n&bull; Main memory space is synchronised to program size. using virtual memory that uses secondary<br />\nstorage to extend the apparent limited size of main memory.<br />\n&bull; Main memory speed is synchronised to CPU speed using high speed cache memory.</p>"},{"n":3,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • ELECTRONICS ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>A particular parallel program computation requires 120 seconds when executed on a single processor. If 80 percent of this computation is ‘inherently sequential’, then what are the theoretically best elapsed times for this program running with 2 and 4 processors respectively?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"20 sec and 100 sec"},{"key":"B","html":"30 sec and 90 sec"},{"key":"C","html":"108 sec and 102 sec"},{"key":"D","html":"80 sec and 40 sec"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(c)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/26/26c3a67c6281e86d3bc5.png\" /></div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/25/2585af8fe052d496ff67.png\" /></div>"},{"n":4,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • ELECTRONICS ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>Which of the following are the memory performance parameters?<br />\n1. Cycle time and bandwidth.<br />\n2. Block size and block access time.<br />\n3. Access time and latency.<br />\nSelect the correct answer from the codes given below:</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"1 only"},{"key":"B","html":"1 and 2 only"},{"key":"C","html":"2 and 3 only"},{"key":"D","html":"1, 2 and 3 only"}],"answer":["D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"D","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(d)<br />\nAll are memory performance parameters.</p>"},{"n":5,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • ELECTRONICS ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>Four processors with their respective process IDs given by <em>P</em><sub>1</sub>, <em>P</em><sub>2</sub>, <em>P</em><sub>3</sub> and <em>P</em><sub>4</sub> having estimated completion time of 8 ms, 6 ms, 4 ms, 2 ms respectively, enter a ready queue together in the order <em>P</em><sub>1</sub>, <em>P</em><sub>2</sub>, <em>P</em><sub>3</sub> and <em>P</em><sub>4</sub>. What is the average turn time in the Round Robin scheduling Algorithm with time 2 ms?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"12.5 msec"},{"key":"B","html":"11.5 msec"},{"key":"C","html":"15 msec"},{"key":"D","html":"13.5 msec"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(c)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/a7/a7e3fb04884eac309fb1.png\" /></div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/3b/3b9f90735a6dc85edf88.png\" /></div>"},{"n":6,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • ELECTRONICS ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>A virtual memory system has an address space of 8 K words, a memory space 4 K words, and page and block sizes of 1 K words. The number of page faults using LRU policy, for following page references is<br />\n1, 2, 3, 0, 4, 6, 2, 1, 3, 4, 2, 0</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"7"},{"key":"B","html":"11"},{"key":"C","html":"9"},{"key":"D","html":"10"}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(b)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/a9/a947665f734eb427af26.png\" /></div>"},{"n":7,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • ELECTRONICS ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>Consider the following schedules for transactions <em>T</em><sub>1</sub>, <em>T</em><sub>2</sub>, <em>T</em><sub>3</sub> and <em>T</em><sub>4</sub>:</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img  src=\"media/72/721e69b477aa3cb3e2c0.png\" /></div>\n\n<div>Which one of the schedules below is the correct serialization of the above?</div>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"<em>T</em><sub>2</sub> – <em>T</em><sub>3</sub> – <em>T</em><sub>4</sub> – <em>T</em><sub>1</sub>"},{"key":"B","html":"<em>T</em><sub>4</sub> – <em>T</em><sub>1</sub> – <em>T</em><sub>3</sub> – <em>T</em><sub>2</sub>"},{"key":"C","html":"<em>T</em><sub>1</sub> – <em>T</em><sub>4</sub> – <em>T</em><sub>2</sub> – <em>T</em><sub>3</sub>"},{"key":"D","html":"<em>T</em><sub>3</sub> – <em>T</em><sub>2</sub> – <em>T</em><sub>1</sub> – <em>T</em><sub>4</sub>"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(c)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/c0/c0b3e91d4a46f4dfe6e3.png\" /></div>"},{"n":8,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • ELECTRONICS ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>Spooling help’s because</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"Print jobs go more smoothly with less stop and go."},{"key":"B","html":"It is more secure method of accessing data."},{"key":"C","html":"The computer is released to do other things while still printing."},{"key":"D","html":"None of these"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(c)<br />\nSpooling is the mechanism of putting jobs in buffer. It uses buffer to hold jobs for a device until it<br />\nis ready to accept the job. This increase the degree of multiprogramming. Example &ndash; Printer<br />\nspooling allows the computer to continue working on other tasks while the printer is busy printing.</p>"},{"n":9,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • ELECTRONICS ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>Aging is</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"Keeping track of how many times a given page is referenced."},{"key":"B","html":"Keeping track of cache contents."},{"key":"C","html":"Keeping track of what pages are currently residing in the memory."},{"key":"D","html":"Increasing the priority of jobs to ensure termination in a finite time."}],"answer":["D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"D","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(d)<br />\nAging is a process that increases the priority of jobs corresponding to the time, process is waiting<br />\nfor the CPU. So, the process with longer waiting time turns to have more priority over the<br />\nprocess that is just arriving. This is done to ensure there is no STARVATION and termination of<br />\nprocesses in a finite time.</p>"},{"n":10,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • ELECTRONICS ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>The Si Darlington transistor pair shown in figure has negligible leakage current, and β<sub>1</sub> = β<sub>2</sub> = 50.<br />\nLet V<sub>CC</sub> = 12 V, <em>R<sub>E</sub></em> = 1 kΩ and <em>R</em><sub>2</sub> → ∞. What is the value of <em>R</em><sub>1</sub> needed to bias the circuit so that <em>V</em><sub>CEQ2</sub> = 6 V.</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"3 MΩ"},{"key":"B","html":"2 MΩ"},{"key":"C","html":"4 MΩ"},{"key":"D","html":"2.5 MΩ"}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(b)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/46/46c1705c1a9ebacdf6e2.png\" /></div>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>"},{"n":11,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • ELECTRONICS ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>The major advantage of cascode configuration is</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"Increased output resistance and wider frequency response while maintaining high voltage gain."},{"key":"B","html":"Increased output resistance and narrow frequency response while maintaining high voltage gain."},{"key":"C","html":"Reduced output resistance and wider frequency response while maintaining high voltage gain."},{"key":"D","html":"None of these."}],"answer":["A"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(a)<br />\nCascode amplifier consists of a common-emitter stage feeding into a common-base stage. It<br />\nachieves high bandwidth by reducing miller capacitance effect. The high gain is achieved by the<br />\ncommon emitter stage and the common base stage acts as an impedance converter, transforming<br />\nlow output impedance into high output resistance.</p>"},{"n":12,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • ELECTRONICS ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>What is the small-signal voltage gain of the circuit as shown in figure? It is assumed that the two transistors are not matched.</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img  src=\"media/d2/d2a39eccb9e072d3143a.png\" /></div>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"17.32"},{"key":"B","html":"–17.32"},{"key":"C","html":"1.732"},{"key":"D","html":"–1.732"}],"answer":["D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"D","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(d)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/de/de019efea26308321173.png\" /></div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/50/50f4b4331bf2b97ba89a.png\" /></div>"},{"n":13,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • ELECTRONICS ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>Which of the following statements is/are correct regarding negative feedback?<br />\n1. It is used in special circuit like bootstrapping.<br />\n2. More number of amplifier stages are required to be used, in order to obtain the required gain.<br />\n3. Decrease in output resistance in case of current shunt and current series feedback amplifier.<br />\n4. It is used in the regulated power supplies.</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"1, 2 and 3 only"},{"key":"B","html":"2, 3 and 4 only"},{"key":"C","html":"1, 2 and 4 only"},{"key":"D","html":"1, 2, 3 and 4"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(c)<br />\nThe output resistance is increased in case of current shunt and current series feedback amplifier.</p>"},{"n":14,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • ELECTRONICS ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>In a RC phase shift oscillator <em>R</em><sub>1</sub> = <em>R</em><sub>2</sub> = <em>R</em><sub>3</sub> = 800 kΩ and <em>C</em><sub>1</sub> = <em>C</em><sub>2</sub> = <em>C</em><sub>3</sub> = 100 pF. The frequency of oscillation is</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"81.2 Hz"},{"key":"B","html":"812 Hz"},{"key":"C","html":"812 Hz"},{"key":"D","html":"8.12 kHz"}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(b)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/3b/3b70951b189b08b3f333.png\" /></div>"},{"n":15,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • ELECTRONICS ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>What is the output voltage <em>V</em><sub>0</sub> for the given circuit?</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img  src=\"media/58/58cf73f491f791ebc84b.png\" /></div>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"6.84 V"},{"key":"B","html":"–6.84 V"},{"key":"C","html":"68.4 V"},{"key":"D","html":"–68.4 V"}],"answer":["A"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(a)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/23/23978a1893073a682e95.png\" /></div>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>"},{"n":16,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • ELECTRONICS ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>Which of the following statements is/are incorrect regarding instrumentation amplifier?<br />\n1. High gain stability with low temperature coefficient.<br />\n2. High DC offset.<br />\n3. High CMRR.<br />\n4. Low gain accuracy.</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"1 and 2 only"},{"key":"B","html":"2 and 4 only"},{"key":"C","html":"2, 3 and 4 only"},{"key":"D","html":"1, 2 and 4 only"}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(b)<br />\nFor accurate measurements, an instrumentation amplifier should have<br />\n&bull; Low DC offset<br />\n&bull; High gain accuracy</p>"},{"n":17,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • ELECTRONICS ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>For the Schmitt trigger circuit shown in figure below, assume <em>R<sub>A</sub></em> = 10 kΩ, <em>R<sub>B</sub></em> = 20 kΩ, <em>R</em><sub>1</sub> = 5 kΩ, <em>R</em><sub>2</sub> = 20 kΩ, Vref = 4 V and saturated output voltages to be ±10 V. Which of these below statements is/are correct? (Assuming op-amp to be ideal)</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img  src=\"media/e8/e833eb774e839f10d9b4.png\" /></div>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div>1. The lower triggering point (LTP) is –5 V.<br />\n2. The upper triggering point (UTP) is 2 V.<br />\n3. The value of <em>V</em><sub>in</sub>, if <em>V</em><sub>0</sub> = –10 V, is –5 V.<br />\n4. Width of hysteresis loop is 6 V.</div>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"1, 2 and 3 only"},{"key":"B","html":"2, 3 and 4 only"},{"key":"C","html":"1, 3 and 4 only"},{"key":"D","html":"1, 2, 3 and 4"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(c)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/c7/c7084cbdf5733ce36e87.png\" /></div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/5d/5de69d36f8fecd2b9d0f.png\" /></div>"},{"n":18,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • ELECTRONICS ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>Which of the following statements is/are correct regarding differential amplifier?<br />\n1. Low offset voltage and current.<br />\n2. Low output impedance.<br />\n3. Low bandwidth<br />\n4. High Common mode gain<br />\n5. Low differential voltage gain</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"1 and 2 only"},{"key":"B","html":"2, 3, 4 only"},{"key":"C","html":"2, 4 and 5 only"},{"key":"D","html":"1, 2, 3 and 5"}],"answer":["A"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(a)<br />\nA differential amplifier should have<br />\n&bull; High bandwidth.<br />\n&bull; Low common mode gain.<br />\n&bull; High differential voltage gain.<br />\n&bull; High input impedance<br />\n&bull; Low output impedance<br />\n&bull; Low offset voltage and current<br />\n&bull; High CMRR</p>"},{"n":19,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • ELECTRONICS ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>For the given K-map, the Boolean expression can be represented in the POS form as</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img  src=\"media/7f/7f530d937b5205dad488.png\" /></div>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"πM(0, 2, 5, 7, 8, 10, 11, 15)"},{"key":"B","html":"Σm(0, 2, 4, 7, 9, 11, 12, 14)"},{"key":"C","html":"Σm(1, 3, 4, 7, 8, 10, 11, 15)"},{"key":"D","html":"πM(1, 3, 4, 6, 9, 11, 12, 14)"}],"answer":["D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"D","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(d)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/02/0283cc13909c5d1dda1a.png\" /></div>"},{"n":20,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • ELECTRONICS ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>T flip-flop is commonly used as</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"a digital counter only."},{"key":"B","html":"a delay switch."},{"key":"C","html":"a digital counter and frequency divider."},{"key":"D","html":"None of these"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(c)<br />\nT flip-flops are often used in digital circuits for applications such as counters and frequency<br />\ndividers because they can toggle their state (change from 0 to 1 or 1 to 0) in response to clock<br />\npulses, making them useful for dividing the frequency of a clock signal or counting events.</p>"},{"n":21,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • ELECTRONICS ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>For the initial state of 000, the function performed by the arrangement of the J-K flip-flops in the given figure is</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img  src=\"media/c7/c7933a6f003566915fd5.png\" /></div>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"Mod-3 counter"},{"key":"B","html":"Mod-6 counter"},{"key":"C","html":"Shift register"},{"key":"D","html":"Mod-2 counter"}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(b)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/bc/bcbd034d81bc4ee2d04a.png\" /></div>"},{"n":22,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • ELECTRONICS ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>Consider, a four variable Boolean function, which contains half the number of minterms with odd number of 1’s. Then the Boolean function can be realized with variables <em>A</em>, <em>B</em>, <em>C</em>, <em>D</em> as:</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"<img  src=\"media/74/7401e1d5bc5589b1ddd8.png\" />"},{"key":"B","html":"<img  src=\"media/ca/cafa4d844d2836dfc64e.png\" />"},{"key":"C","html":"<img  src=\"media/58/587129f19bb066012559.png\" />"},{"key":"D","html":"<img  src=\"media/d1/d154b8c415d16c43aa31.png\" />"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(c)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/3d/3dd0629e1e635e2f9cc2.png\" /></div>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>"},{"n":23,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • ELECTRONICS ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>Which of the following statement(s) is/are correct about CMOS?<br />\n1. CMOS logic inverter has maximum signal swing of 0 V to <em>V<sub>DD</sub></em>.<br />\n2. The output signal swing is independent of exact value of aspect ratio and other device parameters.<br />\n3. It has zero input resistance and infinite output resistance.<br />\n4. It is a fast switching device with wide noise margins.</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"1, 2 and 3 only"},{"key":"B","html":"2, 3 and 4 only"},{"key":"C","html":"1, 2 and 4 only"},{"key":"D","html":"2, 3 and 4 only"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(c)<br />\nCMOS has high input impedance and low output impedance.</p>"},{"n":24,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • ELECTRONICS ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>The state diagram of a Mealy machine is as below:</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img  src=\"media/45/45ec5791442ba355713a.png\" /></div>\n\n<div>Which of the below input sequence(s) (starting from left) generate a high output for more than one clock cycle?<br />\n1. 1010001<br />\n2. 1011110<br />\n3. 1001101<br />\n4. 1010101</div>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"1 and 2 only"},{"key":"B","html":"2 and 3 only"},{"key":"C","html":"3 and 4 only"},{"key":"D","html":"1 and 4 only"}],"answer":["A"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(a)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/96/969a82b6553e183cade5.png\" /></div>"},{"n":25,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • ELECTRONICS ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>Which of the following are not self complementing codes?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"Excess-3 code"},{"key":"B","html":"BCD code"},{"key":"C","html":"2421 binary weighted code"},{"key":"D","html":"4221 binary weighted code"}],"answer":["A"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(a)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/94/94fea2bf814b65a6f489.png\" /></div>"},{"n":26,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • ELECTRONICS ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>Which of the following represent (<em>AB</em>)<sub>16</sub>?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"(01100010)<sub>2</sub> + (01000001)<sub>2</sub>"},{"key":"B","html":"(11000011)<sub>2</sub> – (00111000)<sub>2</sub>"},{"key":"C","html":"(96)<sub>16</sub> + (18)<sub>16</sub>"},{"key":"D","html":"(D3)<sub>16</sub> – (28)<sub>16</sub>"}],"answer":["D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"D","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(d)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/74/74bc9a9da85620417a01.png\" /></div>"},{"n":27,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • ELECTRONICS ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>A particular 8-bit DAC has a full scale output rated at 1.5 V. Its accuracy is ±0.1% of full scale voltage and it has an offset error of ±2 mV. The maximum error is</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"±2.5 mV"},{"key":"B","html":"±4.5 mV"},{"key":"C","html":"±3.5 mV"},{"key":"D","html":"±1.5 mV"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(c)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/a5/a58069d1a35baf468ba8.png\" /></div>"},{"n":28,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • ELECTRONICS ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>Transferred electron mechanism involved in Gunn diode consists of transfer of electrons</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"From valence band to conduction band."},{"key":"B","html":"From valence band to satellite valley."},{"key":"C","html":"From central valley to satellite valley."},{"key":"D","html":"From satellite valley to central valley."}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(c)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/66/66526ee1d6b799932574.png\" /></div>"},{"n":29,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • ELECTRONICS ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>For highly doped diode,</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"Zener breakdown is likely to take place."},{"key":"B","html":"Avalanche breakdown is likely to take place."},{"key":"C","html":"Either (a) or (b) will take place."},{"key":"D","html":"Neither (a) or (b) will take place."}],"answer":["A"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(a)<br />\nWhen the p and n region are heavily doped, direct rupture of covalent bond takes place because<br />\nof the strong electric field, at the junction of pn-diodes. The new electron-hole pairs created<br />\nincrease the reverse current in a reverse biased pn diode causing zener breakdown whereas<br />\nAvalanche breakdown occurs due to collision of high-velocity carriers with lattice atoms, creating<br />\nadditional electron-hole pairs.</p>"},{"n":30,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • ELECTRONICS ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>The leakage current of a p-n diode is caused by</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"heat energy"},{"key":"B","html":"chemical energy"},{"key":"C","html":"barrier potential"},{"key":"D","html":"majority carrier"}],"answer":["A"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(a)<br />\nIn the reverse bias, due to thermal energy or heat energy, minority carriers are generated i.e.<br />\nholes in the n-type region and electrons in p-type region of the semiconductor. The battery<br />\ndrives those minority carriers across the junction, thereby producing a small current called reverse<br />\nsaturation (<em>I</em><sub>0</sub>) or leakage current.</p>"},{"n":31,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • ELECTRONICS ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>For a n-channel JFET, match List-I with List-II and select the correct answer from the codes given below the lists:</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img  src=\"media/74/74bac411954f73735986.png\" /></div>\n\n<div> </div>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"a"},{"key":"B","html":"b"},{"key":"C","html":"c"},{"key":"D","html":"d"}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(b)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/5b/5b5b11cf4f6e154bdb59.png\" /></div>"},{"n":32,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • ELECTRONICS ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>Consider a compensated semiconductor The thermal equilibrium electron and hole concentration in the compensated semiconductor is given by at <em>T</em> = 300 K in which <em>N<sub>a</sub></em> = 10<sup>16</sup>/cm<sup>3</sup> and <em>N<sub>d</sub></em> = 3 × 10<sup>15</sup>/cm<sup>3</sup>. Assume <em>n<sub>i</sub></em> = 1.5 × 10<sup>10</sup>/cm<sup>3</sup>.</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"<img  src=\"media/ab/ab9233e509fb1afcc65e.png\" />"},{"key":"B","html":"<img  src=\"media/45/45bccd723bcf0135fc9f.png\" />"},{"key":"C","html":"<img  src=\"media/6a/6a329dac808f4e910a87.png\" />"},{"key":"D","html":"<img  src=\"media/2a/2ae6abbdb7d6e1adc7b1.png\" />"}],"answer":["A"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(a)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/93/935801871b74b80e3a77.png\" /></div>"},{"n":33,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • ELECTRONICS ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>Consider a silicon pn junction at T = 300 K with doping concentration of <em>N<sub>a</sub></em> = 10<sup>16</sup>/cm<sup>3</sup> and <em>N<sub>d</sub></em> = 10<sup>15</sup>/cm<sup>3</sup>. Assume that <em>n</em><sub>i</sub> = 1.5 × 10<sup>10</sup>/cm<sup>3</sup>. The width of the built in potential barrier in a pn junction is given by</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"0.0637 V"},{"key":"B","html":"0.315 V"},{"key":"C","html":"6.37 V"},{"key":"D","html":"0.637 V"}],"answer":["D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"D","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(d)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/6e/6e8397d01a76d5890774.png\" /></div>"},{"n":34,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • ELECTRONICS ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>Compared to a p-n junction with N<sub>A</sub> = N<sub>D</sub> = 10<sup>15</sup>/cm<sup>3</sup>, which one of the following statements is true for a p-n junction with N<sub>A</sub> = N<sub>D</sub> = 10<sup>21</sup>/cm<sup>3</sup>,?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"Reverse breakdown voltage is higher and depletion capacitance is lower."},{"key":"B","html":"Reverse breakdown voltage is lower and depletion capacitance is higher."},{"key":"C","html":"Reverse breakdown voltage is lower and depletion capacitance is lower."},{"key":"D","html":"Reverse breakdown voltage is higher and depletion capacitance is higher."}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(b)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/3d/3d93f1516578da3012ec.png\" /></div>"},{"n":35,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • ELECTRONICS ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>Which of the following statements is/are correct?<br />\n1. The threshold voltage (<em>V<sub>T</sub></em>) of a MOS capacitor decreases with increase in gate oxide thickness.<br />\n2. The threshold voltage (<em>V<sub>T</sub></em>) of a MOS capacitor decreases with increase in substrate doping concentration.<br />\n3. By using the n-type poly Si crystalline material as gate, the threshold voltage can be reduced.<br />\n4. Because of the difference in the Wwrk function of the metal and semiconductor, the flatband voltage is zero.</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"1, 2 and 3 only"},{"key":"B","html":"2, 3 and 4 only"},{"key":"C","html":"1, 3 and 4 only"},{"key":"D","html":"1, 2, 3 and 4 only"}],"answer":["A"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(a)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/83/836f89c0cfe918330c43.png\" /></div>"},{"n":36,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • ELECTRONICS ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>Consider a reverse biased long p-n diode with the following data:<br />\nWidth of depletion layer, W = 1.5 μm, Carrier Generation Rate, <em>G</em> = 10<sup>20</sup>/cm<sup>3</sup> sec, <em>D<sub>n</sub></em> = 90 cm<sup>2</sup>/sec,</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div>\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img  src=\"media/7d/7d37d17b77d7d582ea72.png\" /></div>\n</div>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"13.0 A/cm<sup>2</sup>"},{"key":"B","html":"0.130 A/cm<sup>2</sup>"},{"key":"C","html":"1.30 A/cm<sup>2</sup>"},{"key":"D","html":"130.4 A/cm<sup>2</sup>"}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(b)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/57/57e97b57b19d226df8a0.png\" /></div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/96/96dc80e6cda063139e78.png\" /></div>"},{"n":37,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • ELECTRONICS ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>Consider the following statements:<br />\n1. An auto transformer has higher efficiency than a two winding transformer of same rating.<br />\n2. In an auto transformer, power is transferred through both conduction and induction processes.<br />\n3. In an auto transformer, power is transferred through induction process only.<br />\nWhich of the above statements are correct?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"1 and 2"},{"key":"B","html":"2 and 3"},{"key":"C","html":"1 and 3"},{"key":"D","html":"1, 2 and 3"}],"answer":["A"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(a)<br />\n&bull; An autotransformer has higher efficiency than two winding transformer because of low ohmic<br />\nloss and core loss due to the reduction of transformer material.<br />\n&bull; In auto-transformer, there is a common electrical path between primary and secondary, so<br />\npower is transferred through both conduction and induction process.</p>"},{"n":38,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • ELECTRONICS ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>A 4 kVA, 400/200 V, single phase-transformer has resistance of 0.02 p.u. and reactance of 0.06 p.u. What is the value of its resistance and reactance respectively referred to h.v. side?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"0.2 Ω and 0.6 Ω"},{"key":"B","html":"1 Ω and 3 Ω"},{"key":"C","html":"0.08 Ω and 0.24 Ω"},{"key":"D","html":"0.8 Ω and 2.4 Ω"}],"answer":["D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"D","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(d)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/d1/d183c9773770ab9a8dec.png\" /></div>"},{"n":39,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • ELECTRONICS ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>If the applied voltage to a d.c. machine is 250 V, then back emf for maximum power developed is</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"62.5 V"},{"key":"B","html":"125 V"},{"key":"C","html":"250 V"},{"key":"D","html":"500 V"}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(b)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/5f/5f9691a18b593274d4c6.png\" /></div>"},{"n":40,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • ELECTRONICS ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>The speed-current characteristics of different d.c. motors are shown below. Which of the following options correctly matches the characteristics with the respective d.c. motors?</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img  src=\"media/05/05d451105bab66ad69e8.png\" /></div>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"I - Compound, II - Shunt, III - Series"},{"key":"B","html":"I - Shunt, II - Series, III - Compound"},{"key":"C","html":"I - Compound, II - Series, III - Shunt"},{"key":"D","html":"I - Shunt, II - Compound, III - Series"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(c)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/c5/c540281b62c5a55cd098.png\" /></div>"},{"n":41,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • ELECTRONICS ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>Match <strong>List-I</strong> with <strong>List-II</strong> and select the correct answer using the codes given below the lists:</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img  src=\"media/96/9604b01040fed2b8580c.png\" /></div>\n\n<div> </div>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"a"},{"key":"B","html":"b"},{"key":"C","html":"c"},{"key":"D","html":"d"}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(b)<br />\n&bull; In 3-phase slip ring induction motor, a star connected variable resistance is connected in the rotor circuit through slip-rings, thereby increasing the rotor resistance which improves the starting torque.<br />\n&bull; In a 3-phase squirrel cage induction motor with an autotransformer starter, an autotransformer<br />\nis used to control the voltage applied to the motor during the starting process.<br />\n&bull; When single phase AC supply is given to stator winding of single-phase motor, it produces<br />\nalternating flux i.e. which alternates along one space axis only. It is not synchronously revolving<br />\n(or rotating) flux, thus it cannot produce any rotation. Hence single phase induction motor is not self-starting.</p>"},{"n":42,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • ELECTRONICS ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>A 6 pole, 60 Hz, <img align=\"absmiddle\"  src=\"media/18/18c0d5175b8df20c334c.png\" /> induction motor running on full load develops a useful torque of 150 Nm at a rotor frequency of 3 Hz. The shaft power output is______.</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"178.98 kW"},{"key":"B","html":"1.7898 kW"},{"key":"C","html":"17.898 kW"},{"key":"D","html":"0.1789 kW"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(c)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/04/04f9950e02b65db1de00.png\" /></div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/b3/b39eb500772a8938880f.png\" /></div>"},{"n":43,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • ELECTRONICS ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>Consider the following:<br />\n1. Supply voltage<br />\n2. Excitation current<br />\n3. Maximum value of load angle<br />\nThe maximum power developed by a synchronous motor is a function of which of the above?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"1 and 3"},{"key":"B","html":"1 and 2"},{"key":"C","html":"2 and 3"},{"key":"D","html":"1, 2 and 3"}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(b)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/a0/a0fecafd6dcfcba11efd.png\" /></div>"},{"n":44,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • ELECTRONICS ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>Which of the following statements is/are correct?<br />\n1. Turbine and alternator are mechanically coupled with each other.<br />\n2. Alternator convert mechanical energy of turbine into electrical energy.<br />\n3. In thermal generating stations coal, oil natural gas etc. are employed as secondary sources of energy.<br />\n4. Inside a nuclear power station, energy is released by nuclear fission in the core of the reactor.</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"1, 2 and 3"},{"key":"B","html":"2, 3 and 4"},{"key":"C","html":"1, 2 and 4"},{"key":"D","html":"1, 2, 3 and 4"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(c)<br />\nIn thermal generating stations coal, oil, natural gas etc. are employed as primary source of energy.</p>"},{"n":45,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • ELECTRONICS ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>The torque angle of a synchronous machine operating from a constant voltage bus, is usually defined as the space angle between</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"Rotor mmf wave and resultant flux-density wave."},{"key":"B","html":"Rotor mmf wave and stator mmf wave."},{"key":"C","html":"Stator mmf wave and resultant flux density wave."},{"key":"D","html":"Stator mmf wave and resultant mmf wave."}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(b)<br />\nThe angle between stator mmf and rotor mmf is called torque angle. The angle between the rotor mmf and the resultant mmf is called load angle.</p>"},{"n":46,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • ELECTRONICS ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>In the circuit shown in the figure, the network is described by the SC parameters:</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img  src=\"media/4a/4a12fa311265763ce47c.png\" /></div>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div>Which of the following results obtained are correct?<br />\n1. <em>V</em><sub>1</sub> = 68.57 V<br />\n2. <em>V</em><sub>2</sub> = –114.285 V</div>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"1 only"},{"key":"B","html":"2 only"},{"key":"C","html":"Both 1 and 2"},{"key":"D","html":"Neither 1 nor 2"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(c)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/ab/ab8f7abbe9afd2d94c86.png\" /></div>"},{"n":47,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • ELECTRONICS ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>A two element series circuit is having current <img align=\"absmiddle\"  src=\"media/94/940ff5b41f236b66c4a1.png\" /> = 4.24 cos (5000t + 45°)A, power 180 W and power factor 0.80 lagging. The elements of the circuit are given by [cos<sup>–1</sup>(0.80) = 36.87°, tan 36.87° = 0.75]</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"<em>C</em> = 5 μF, <em>R</em> = 20 Ω"},{"key":"B","html":"<em>C</em> = 5 mF, <em>R</em> = 10 Ω"},{"key":"C","html":"<em>L</em> = 3 mH, <em>R</em> = 20 Ω"},{"key":"D","html":"<em>L</em> = 3 mH, <em>R</em> = 10 Ω"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(c)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/d8/d82b39553d3369aa2b6d.png\" /></div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/46/46ff3522a745479a4c6e.png\" /></div>"},{"n":48,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • ELECTRONICS ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>What is the value of <img align=\"absmiddle\"  src=\"media/6d/6d74d235bcf6ab03f6e5.png\" /> in the below figure?</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img  src=\"media/6b/6b31d023d2d73aba8fa6.png\" /></div>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"5"},{"key":"B","html":"12"},{"key":"C","html":"13"},{"key":"D","html":"15"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(c)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/7e/7e41e32e4053fb565320.png\" /></div>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>"},{"n":49,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • ELECTRONICS ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>The Thevenin’s equivalent of the network below can be represented as</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img  src=\"media/63/63e197e151920e2fb9a8.png\" /></div>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"<img  src=\"media/99/996339846c97b7a13c88.png\" />"},{"key":"B","html":"<img  src=\"media/25/2538ab7370c134a38df7.png\" />"},{"key":"C","html":"<img  src=\"media/3e/3e4506e4f509778767dc.png\" />"},{"key":"D","html":"<img  src=\"media/00/00eb1b07be6ba9b3d246.png\" />"}],"answer":["A"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(a)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/50/50c4d5dd013bdabcda25.png\" /></div>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>"},{"n":50,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • ELECTRONICS ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>A series RC circuit with <em>R</em> = 5 kΩ and C = 20 μF has a constant voltage source of 100 V applied at <em>t</em> = 0. Assuming there is no initial charge on the capacitor, the voltage across the resistance <em>V<sub>R</sub></em> for <em>t</em> > 0 is given by</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"–100<em>e</em><sup>–10<em>t</em></sup> V"},{"key":"B","html":"–10<em>e</em><sup>–0.1t</sup> V"},{"key":"C","html":"100<em>e</em><sup>–10<em>t</em></sup> V"},{"key":"D","html":"10<em>e</em><sup>–0.1t</sup> V"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(c)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/41/41a8908d51eac00c4e54.png\" /></div>"},{"n":51,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • ELECTRONICS ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>When the total charge in a capacitor is doubled, the energy stored</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"remains the same"},{"key":"B","html":"is halved"},{"key":"C","html":"is doubled"},{"key":"D","html":"is quadrupled"}],"answer":["D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"D","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(d)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/9f/9f946ae407ef28697fa6.png\" /></div>"},{"n":52,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • ELECTRONICS ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>The process of increasing the power factor without altering the voltage or current to the original load is known as _________.</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"varmeter"},{"key":"B","html":"power measurement"},{"key":"C","html":"power factor correction"},{"key":"D","html":"improved power factor"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(c)<br />\nPower factor correction involves the addition of capacitors to the electrical system, which compensates for the reactive power and improves the overall power factor without changing the voltage or current supplied to the original load.</p>"},{"n":53,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • ELECTRONICS ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>The rule for eliminating the transformer and reflecting the primary circuit to the secondary side is given by (Assume <em>n</em> is the transformer turns ratio)<br />\n1. Multiply the primary impedance by <em>n</em><sup>2</sup>.<br />\n2. Multiply the primary voltage by <em>n</em>.<br />\n3. Divide the primary current by <em>n</em>.</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"1, 2"},{"key":"B","html":"2, 3"},{"key":"C","html":"1, 2, 3"},{"key":"D","html":"1, 3"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(c)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/e4/e4decc37867fc2eec01c.png\" /></div>"},{"n":54,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • ELECTRONICS ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>Which of the following statements is/are correct?<br />\n1. To apply reciprocity theorem, unit of response to excitation should be either \u0002<img align=\"absmiddle\"  src=\"media/c4/c4d6111b90f6a3ffb1b2.png\" /> or Ω.<br />\n2. While applying “Reciprocity theorem”, only one independent source is activated.<br />\n3. When the network is having dependent source, reciprocity theorem cannot be applied.</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"1, 2"},{"key":"B","html":"1, 2, 3"},{"key":"C","html":"2, 3"},{"key":"D","html":"1, 3"}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(b)</p>"},{"n":55,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • ELECTRONICS ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>Which of the following statements are correct?<br />\n1. Bode plot analysis is suitable only for minimum phase transfer function whereas Nyquist plot<br />\nmethod is suitable for both minimum and non-minimum phase transfer functions.<br />\n2. If open loop system is minimum phase system, then for closed loop system to be stable, both<br />\ngain margin and phase margin should be positive.<br />\n3. The magnitude of resonant peak <em>M<sub>r</sub></em>, gives an indication of the relative stability of the system.</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"1, 2"},{"key":"B","html":"1, 2, 3"},{"key":"C","html":"2, 3"},{"key":"D","html":"1, 3"}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(b)<br />\n&bull; Bode plot analysis is suitable only for minimum phase transfer function whereas Nyquist plot method is suitable for both minimum and non-minimum phase transfer functions.<br />\n&bull; For the system to be stable, both Gain Margin and Phase Margin should be positive<br />\n&bull; The magnitude of the resonant peak gives an indication of the relative stability of the system.<br />\nA large resonant peak magnitude indicates the presence of a pair of dominant closed-loop<br />\npoles with small damping ratio, which will yield an undesirable transient response. A smaller<br />\nresonant peak magnitude, on the other hand, indicates the absence of a pair of dominant<br />\nclosed-loop poles with small damping ratio, meaning that the system is well damped.</p>"},{"n":56,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • ELECTRONICS ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>The open-loop transfer function of a feedback control system is <img align=\"absmiddle\"  src=\"media/87/87ce6333b13d9e57f19f.png\" /> The gain margin of the system is</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"16"},{"key":"B","html":"8"},{"key":"C","html":"4"},{"key":"D","html":"2"}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(b)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/f9/f92f1974bdd64fbedc77.png\" /></div>"},{"n":57,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • ELECTRONICS ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>The loop transfer function of a system is given by</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img  src=\"media/34/341cbdcc3ef196074669.png\" /></div>\n\n<div>The phase crossover frequency is 5 rad/sec. The value of the dead time <em>L</em> is</div>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"<img align=\"absmiddle\"  src=\"media/e9/e99a4f995c8738d92438.png\" />"},{"key":"B","html":"<img align=\"absmiddle\"  src=\"media/91/916960eceff1d50a7921.png\" />"},{"key":"C","html":"<img align=\"absmiddle\"  src=\"media/ab/abee63f7215cfab1d628.png\" />"},{"key":"D","html":"zero"}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(b)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/bf/bf910390ec8b6289c067.png\" /></div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/77/778a6e0a4b0fe92de0e6.png\" /></div>"},{"n":58,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • ELECTRONICS ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>If a first order system and its time response to a unit step input are as shown in the following figure, then the gain <em>K</em> is</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img  src=\"media/b4/b45d51618f0d9778853e.png\" /></div>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"0.25"},{"key":"B","html":"0.8"},{"key":"C","html":"1"},{"key":"D","html":"4"}],"answer":["D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"D","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(d)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/6c/6c1fd5f31ec8e6bfd121.png\" /></div>"},{"n":59,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • ELECTRONICS ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>Which of the following statements is/are correct regarding PI controller?<br />\n1. <em>e<sub>ss</sub></em> is reduced, which means the accuracy improves.<br />\n2. The order and type of system increases by 1 that improves damping and reduces overshoot.<br />\n3. The noise is filtered.<br />\n4. The offset is eliminated.</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"1, 2"},{"key":"B","html":"1, 2, 3"},{"key":"C","html":"1, 3, 4"},{"key":"D","html":"All of the above"}],"answer":["D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"D","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(d)<br />\n&bull; PI controller increases the type of the system and thus, steady state error is reduced. It also<br />\neliminates the offset error and also improves the damping, thereby reducing overshoot.<br />\n&bull; PI controller acts as a low pass filter filtering the high-frequency noise.</p>"},{"n":60,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • ELECTRONICS ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>The transfer function for the system described by the signal flow graph given below is</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img  src=\"media/60/6003dd955dccdfc14abb.png\" /></div>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"<img  src=\"media/bc/bc1ade6cfac7ce332926.png\" />"},{"key":"B","html":"<img  src=\"media/13/136cc747ec40e48ca3c6.png\" />"},{"key":"C","html":"<img  src=\"media/df/df5c378b880b19415bf7.png\" />"},{"key":"D","html":"<img  src=\"media/0a/0ac284b92749db14e826.png\" />"}],"answer":["D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"D","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(d)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/d3/d31fc6853ee62604c585.png\" /></div>"},{"n":61,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • ELECTRONICS ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>A unity negative feedback position control system has a forward path transfer function <em>G</em>(<em>s</em>) = <img align=\"absmiddle\"  src=\"media/29/2960ad4cf888fa527fb8.png\" /> . For unit step input, the value of K that minimizes ISE (Integral square error) is</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"<em>K</em> → ∞"},{"key":"B","html":"<em>K</em> → 2"},{"key":"C","html":"<em>K</em> → 0"},{"key":"D","html":"Can’t find"}],"answer":["A"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(a)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/0b/0b53c08c334e7b2f1586.png\" /></div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/7d/7d6f0bd807f031f071d5.png\" /></div>"},{"n":62,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • ELECTRONICS ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>The bode-plot for lag-lead compensator is</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"<img  src=\"media/e7/e7aac7aa48728e1f01e6.png\" />"},{"key":"B","html":"<img  src=\"media/0a/0ac3b29abb6964716d17.png\" />"},{"key":"C","html":"<img  src=\"media/4a/4a39462ca5ee6e86989b.png\" />"},{"key":"D","html":"<img  src=\"media/a6/a6cbe6ebeb003a8d8d41.png\" />"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(c)<br />\nIn a lag-lead compensator, lag system is dominant. Hence, pole is nearer to origin than the zero<br />\ni.e. the corner frequency due to pole is less than corner frequency due to zero.</p>"},{"n":63,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • ELECTRONICS ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>A system with transfer function of <img align=\"absmiddle\"  src=\"media/4e/4e5192b553c1c2f463a3.png\" /> subjected to a step input takes 10 seconds to reach 50% of step height. The value of τ is</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"6.9s"},{"key":"B","html":"10s"},{"key":"C","html":"14.4s"},{"key":"D","html":"20s"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(c)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/3a/3abc5fd20f5314f1aa44.png\" /></div>"},{"n":64,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • ELECTRONICS ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>If excited by a unit step input, the final value of the response y(t) of a system whose transfer function is</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img  src=\"media/42/428e79792b2338c5ee2c.png\" /></div>\n\n<div>is given by</div>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"3"},{"key":"B","html":"<img  src=\"media/c1/c1e29930a3a9e344398d.png\" />"},{"key":"C","html":"0"},{"key":"D","html":"∞"}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(b)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/e6/e63a88984e12563a5253.png\" /></div>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>"},{"n":65,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • ELECTRONICS ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>Which of the following are correct statements?</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img align=\"absmiddle\"  src=\"media/ec/ec480d7ae2a058bf48ba.png\" /></div>\n\n<div>2. The sum of M periodic continuous time signals is not necessarily periodic.<br />\n3. All finite periodic signals are power signals; however, not all power signals are periodic.<br />\n4. If <img  src=\"media/07/07b6a759a73d0721cd8a.png\" /> is the Hilbert transform of g(t), then the Hilbert transform of <img align=\"absmiddle\"  src=\"media/6c/6c55c81c724e0e7c3d94.png\" /> is g(t).</div>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"1, 2, 3, 4"},{"key":"B","html":"1, 3, 4"},{"key":"C","html":"2, 3"},{"key":"D","html":"1, 2, 3"}],"answer":["D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"D","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(d)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/f2/f2b45f3e2064d7b9a3cf.png\" /></div>"},{"n":66,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • ELECTRONICS ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>The following are the impulse responses of continuous time LTI systems:</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img  src=\"media/34/341eea25134392216f4a.png\" /></div>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div>Which of the following represents stable systems?</div>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"1, 2, 3"},{"key":"B","html":"1, 4"},{"key":"C","html":"1, 3, 4"},{"key":"D","html":"1, 2, 4"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(c)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/ca/ca6a29803bd85d00ed2a.png\" /></div>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>"},{"n":67,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • ELECTRONICS ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>Let <img align=\"absmiddle\"  src=\"media/33/33c1a2c56437d68a3ad1.png\" /> be a periodic signal whose Fourier series coefficient are:</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img  src=\"media/5b/5b528e3b850ebbb7525e.png\" /></div>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div>Which of the following is/are correct statements?\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img  src=\"media/6f/6f888046873938a3154f.png\" /></div>\n</div>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"1, 3"},{"key":"B","html":"2, 3"},{"key":"C","html":"only 2"},{"key":"D","html":"only 1"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(c)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/0e/0e8a0d3b9468c66bb3f5.png\" /></div>"},{"n":68,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • ELECTRONICS ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>The analog filter transfer function is given as <em>H</em>(<em>s</em>) = 1/(<em>s</em> + 2). What is the equivalent digital filter function if we use impulse invariant method? [Consider sampling period T = 1 sec] given as</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"<img align=\"absmiddle\"  src=\"media/eb/eb40ec91daf4a371b774.png\" />"},{"key":"B","html":"<img align=\"absmiddle\"  src=\"media/cf/cf7f83aef47b2f7c3604.png\" />"},{"key":"C","html":"<img align=\"absmiddle\"  src=\"media/d6/d6099aaad3077a90d3b8.png\" />"},{"key":"D","html":"<img align=\"absmiddle\"  src=\"media/c2/c2224c312e1ab621c84e.png\" />"}],"answer":["A"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(a)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/bc/bc616391740814307e8d.png\" /></div>"},{"n":69,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • ELECTRONICS ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>The large side lobes of window function, <em>W</em>(<em>e</em><sup>jω</sup>) results in which of the following undesirable effects?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"Circling effects"},{"key":"B","html":"Broading effects"},{"key":"C","html":"Ringing effects"},{"key":"D","html":"Shortening effects"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(c)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/8b/8bd134e199c7b6bb0ed2.png\" /></div>"},{"n":70,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • ELECTRONICS ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>Consider the following statements regarding bilinear transformation:<br />\n1. Shape of magnitude response of analog filter is reserved.<br />\n2. Linear relation between analog and digital frequency.<br />\n3. A continuous time differentiator can’t be converted to a discrete time differentiator.<br />\n4. Phase response curve is non-linear.<br />\nWhich of the above statements is/are correct?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"1 only"},{"key":"B","html":"1, 3, 4 only"},{"key":"C","html":"1 and 4 only"},{"key":"D","html":"1, 2, 3 and 4"}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(b)<br />\nThere is a non-linear relationship between analog and digital frequency in bilinear transformation<br />\nmethod. It introduces distortion, which is called frequency warping.<br />\nSo, statement (2) is false, remaining all are true.</p>"},{"n":71,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • ELECTRONICS ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>In CD playback system, which of the following filter is used to pass frequencies below 20 kHz while blocking frequencies above 88.2 kHz?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"Butterworth"},{"key":"B","html":"Chebyshev"},{"key":"C","html":"Elliptical"},{"key":"D","html":"Bessel"}],"answer":["D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"D","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(d)<br />\nIn CD playback system, Bessel filter is used to pass frequencies below 20 kHz, while blocking frequencies above 88.2 kHz.</p>"},{"n":72,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • ELECTRONICS ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>The Fourier transform of u[1 – |t|] is</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"sinc(<em>f</em>)"},{"key":"B","html":"<img align=\"absmiddle\"  src=\"media/2a/2aab70b4d5a51ec7ce8a.png\" />"},{"key":"C","html":"2 sinc(2<em>f</em>)"},{"key":"D","html":"sinc2(<em>f</em>)"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(c)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/ae/ae1282e78ba1e6f69b96.png\" /></div>"},{"n":73,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • ELECTRONICS ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>Over a certain binary communication channel, the symbol 0 is transmitted with probability 0.4 and symbol 1 is transmitted with probability 0.6. It is given that <em>P</em>(ε/0) = 10<sup>–6</sup> and <em>P</em>(ε/1) = 10<sup>–4</sup>, where <img align=\"absmiddle\"  src=\"media/08/0819a32a17e5f6bd0fbe.png\" /> is the probability of detecting the error given that <img align=\"absmiddle\"  src=\"media/ec/ecd3739f84d2b8f0b703.png\" /> is transmitted. What is <em>P</em>(ε),<br />\nthe error probability of the channel?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"0.8 × 10<sup>–4</sup>"},{"key":"B","html":"0.4 × 10<sup>–4</sup>"},{"key":"C","html":"0.604 × (10<sup>–4</sup>)"},{"key":"D","html":"0.24 × 10<sup>–4</sup>"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(c)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/d1/d11640026616ec6e0991.png\" /></div>"},{"n":74,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • ELECTRONICS ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>What is the mean square value of a random process</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img  src=\"media/59/59b471b5bbab4a08b796.png\" /></div>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div>where θ is a RV uniformly distributed over (0, 2π)?</div>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"<img  src=\"media/cf/cf3149bdb9cbc3badab5.png\" />"},{"key":"B","html":"<img  src=\"media/b4/b4365f3167eb9ec35b71.png\" />"},{"key":"C","html":"<img  src=\"media/7a/7af3681d867415b632e8.png\" />"},{"key":"D","html":"<img  src=\"media/ec/ecaaf12aba42ae0c9aaa.png\" />"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(c)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/ec/ec6f090dcb4968cfed8d.png\" /></div>"},{"n":75,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • ELECTRONICS ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>A DSB modulated signal <img align=\"absmiddle\"  src=\"media/83/83972398c8808d32bb20.png\" /> = A<em><sub>m</sub></em>(<em>t</em>) cos 2π<em>f<sub>c</sub>t</em> is multiplied with a local carrier <em>c</em>(<em>t</em>) = cos(ω<em><sub>c</sub>t</em> + θ) and the output is passed through a LPF with a bandwidth equal to the bandwidth of the message <em>m</em>(<em>t</em>). If the power of the message signal m(t) is <em>P<sub>M</sub></em>, then the power of the signal at the output of the LPF is given by</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"<img align=\"absmiddle\"  src=\"media/5c/5c72b34b8177b90929a7.png\" />"},{"key":"B","html":"<img align=\"absmiddle\"  src=\"media/50/50ad2cc254432940d941.png\" />"},{"key":"C","html":"<img align=\"absmiddle\"  src=\"media/1b/1b20a173ef1e839d0bd8.png\" />"},{"key":"D","html":"None of the above"}],"answer":["D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"D","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(b)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/ee/ee6f92c778dbed6f2fde.png\" /></div>"},{"n":76,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • ELECTRONICS ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>Binary data are transmitted over a noisy communication channel in a block of 16 binary digitsl.<br />\nThe probability that a received digit is in error due to channel noise is 0.01. Assume that the error occurring in various digit positions within a block are independent.<br />\nThe variance of the number of errors per block at the receiver ouput is given by</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"0.16"},{"key":"B","html":"0.986"},{"key":"C","html":"0.158"},{"key":"D","html":"0.014"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(c)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/e8/e8ef14a7805dd148360e.png\" /></div>"},{"n":77,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • ELECTRONICS ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>Consider a narrow band FM signal approximately defined by</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img  src=\"media/45/45391c427dc9e57af625.png\" /></div>\n\n<div>What is the ratio of the maximum to the minimum value of the envelope of the given modulated signal? Assume β = 0.3.</div>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"0.3"},{"key":"B","html":"0.7"},{"key":"C","html":"1.044"},{"key":"D","html":"0.8"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(c)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/44/4424d7d0c0e44ce4ddea.png\" /></div>"},{"n":78,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • ELECTRONICS ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>Which of the following statements is/are correct?<br />\n1. Amplitude modulation is wasteful of power.<br />\n2. Amplitude modulation is wasteful of bandwidth.<br />\n3. The video signal exhibits a large bandwidth and significant low-frequency content, which suggests the use of vestigial sideband modulation.<br />\n4. Envelope detection is based on the principle of non-coherent detection.</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"1, 2"},{"key":"B","html":"1, 2, 3"},{"key":"C","html":"2, 3"},{"key":"D","html":"1, 2, 3, 4"}],"answer":["D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"D","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(d)<br />\nEnvelope detection is based on the principle of non-coherent detection since it does not require that receiver recover the phase of the sinusoidal carrier.<br />\nNon-coherent detection has the advantage that it is simple to implement but has the disadvantage<br />\nthat it is wasteful of power.</p>"},{"n":79,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • ELECTRONICS ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>Which of the following is/are properties of angle modulated waves?<br />\n1. Constancy of transmitted power.<br />\n2. Non linearity of the modulation process.<br />\n3. Irregularity of zero crossings.<br />\n4. Visualization difficulty of message waveform.<br />\n5. Trade-off of increased transmission bandwidth for improved noise performance.</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"1, 2, 3, 4"},{"key":"B","html":"2, 3, 4, 5"},{"key":"C","html":"3, 4, 5, 1"},{"key":"D","html":"1, 2, 3, 4, 5"}],"answer":["D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"D","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(d)<br />\n1. The amplitude of PM and FM wave is maintained at a constant value equal to the carrier<br />\namplitude. Consequently, the average transmitted power of angle modulated waves is a constant.<br />\n2. Both PM and FM waves violate the principle of superposition.<br />\n3. The irregularity of zero crossings in angle modulated waves is also attributed to the nonlinear<br />\ncharacter of the modulation process.<br />\n4. In AM, we see the message waveform as the envelope of the modulated wave. This is not so in angle modulation.<br />\n5. The transmission of a message signal by modulating the angle of a sinusoidal carrier is less<br />\nsensitive to the presence of additive noise than transmission by modulating the amplitude of<br />\ncarrier. The improvement in noise performance is achieved at the expense of a corresponding<br />\nincrease in the transmission bandwidth requirement of angle modulation.</p>"},{"n":80,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • ELECTRONICS ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>Which of the following relations is/are correct for a real random process <em>X</em>(<em>t</em>)?</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img  src=\"media/9d/9deb0f3f243ea6f5f233.png\" /></div>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"1, 2, 3, 4"},{"key":"B","html":"2, 3"},{"key":"C","html":"1, 2"},{"key":"D","html":"1, 2, 3"}],"answer":["D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"D","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(d)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/eb/ebf71f807396ce62207a.png\" /></div>"},{"n":81,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • ELECTRONICS ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>Which of the following statements is/are correct?<br />\n1. The syndrome depends only on the error pattern and not on the transmitted code word.<br />\n2. All error patterns that differ by a code word have the same syndrome.</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"1 only"},{"key":"B","html":"2 only"},{"key":"C","html":"None of the above"},{"key":"D","html":"both are correct"}],"answer":["D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"D","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(d)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/8f/8f141ecb349fd2f96fbb.png\" /></div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/8b/8b2dba8a48a0fb9787e6.png\" /></div>"},{"n":82,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • ELECTRONICS ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>Which of the following statements is/are correct for prevention of latch-up?<br />\n1. Use of <em>p</em><sup>+</sup> and <em>n</em><sup>+</sup> guard rings around nmos and pmos connected to the ground and <em>V<sub>DD </sub></em>respectively.<br />\n2. Reduction of R-well and R substrate as much as possible by placing substrate and well contacts<br />\nas close as possible to the source connection of transistors connected to the supply rails.<br />\n3. Keeping sufficient spacing between the NMOS and PMOS transistors.</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"1 and 2"},{"key":"B","html":"1, 2 and 3"},{"key":"C","html":"2 and 3"},{"key":"D","html":"1 and 3"}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(b)</p>"},{"n":83,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • ELECTRONICS ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>Arrange the following steps of water processing in the correct order:</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img  src=\"media/ee/ee4040ff2d089b3ec012.png\" /></div>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"1, 2, 3 and 4"},{"key":"B","html":"2, 3, 4 and 1"},{"key":"C","html":"1, 3, 4 and 2"},{"key":"D","html":"1, 4, 3 and 2"}],"answer":["D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"D","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(d)<br />\nThe correct order of steps in wafer processing are wafer preparation, oxidation, photolithography,<br />\netching, diffusion, ion implantation, deposition, metallization and packaging.</p>"},{"n":84,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • ELECTRONICS ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>Figure shows a wafer consisting of 12 chip sites with 8 good chips and 4 faulty chips. Cost of fabricating this wafer is $10 and cost of testing this wafer is $2. Then, what is the cost of a chip?</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img  src=\"media/bc/bc7e0a0804237fd1bf29.png\" /></div>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"$ 0.5"},{"key":"B","html":"$ 4"},{"key":"C","html":"$ 2"},{"key":"D","html":"$ 1.5"}],"answer":["D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"D","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(d)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/53/53bc61db8247c6f2d507.png\" /></div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/0d/0def56eed86f51119390.png\" /></div>"},{"n":85,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • ELECTRONICS ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>What is the <img align=\"absmiddle\"  src=\"media/da/da1864fd4a0740edd41f.png\" /> ratios for the transistors in CMOS logic circuit shown below, such that worst case delay of the circuit does not exceed the delay of the basic inverter with <img align=\"absmiddle\"  src=\"media/9f/9f51ab69ce3c29cdb76f.png\" /> and <em>L</em> = 0.25 μm?</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img  src=\"media/90/904e916cd502e6d4976e.png\" /></div>\n\n<div> </div>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"<img  src=\"media/5b/5bd33edd1f6cad1bb54f.png\" />"},{"key":"B","html":"<img  src=\"media/c4/c4ddbe20e7a0ab1648fa.png\" />"},{"key":"C","html":"<img  src=\"media/98/98e9763a846a1611e616.png\" />"},{"key":"D","html":"<img  src=\"media/4d/4d7b737ad8cea30e258e.png\" />"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(c)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/17/1755f3c53c6e633bb44a.png\" /></div>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>"},{"n":86,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • ELECTRONICS ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>Phosphorous is implanted into P-type silicon sample with uniform doping concentration of 10<sup>16</sup> atoms/cm<sup>3</sup>. If the beam current density is 2 μA/cm<sup>2</sup> and implantation is carried out for 10 min, the implantation dose is</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"7.5 × 10<sup>15</sup> cm<sup>–2</sup>"},{"key":"B","html":"0.3 × 10<sup>10 </sup>cm<sup>–2</sup>"},{"key":"C","html":"5 × 10<sup>15</sup> cm<sup>–2</sup>"},{"key":"D","html":"9.97 × 10<sup>15</sup> cm<sup>–2</sup>"}],"answer":["A"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(a)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/97/97a700726001b8a35ac4.png\" /></div>"},{"n":87,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • ELECTRONICS ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>Which of the following expression is represented by the below circuit?</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img  src=\"media/9d/9ddaeba24c458f2f184e.png\" /></div>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"<img  src=\"media/aa/aa68aaa61633593566cd.png\" />"},{"key":"B","html":"<img  src=\"media/96/96163922347f8b442878.png\" />"},{"key":"C","html":"<img  src=\"media/70/7022cef70b6d963a0cef.png\" />"},{"key":"D","html":"None of the above"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(a)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/36/36be6254177f1163c1fa.png\" /></div>"},{"n":88,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • ELECTRONICS ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>What is the <img align=\"absmiddle\"  src=\"media/d2/d2d0d24442adf023a696.png\" /> ratio of the NMOS transistor used in resistive load inverter with <em>V<sub>TO</sub></em> = 1 V, <em>V<sub>DD</sub></em> = 5 V, <em>V</em><sub><em>OL</em> </sub>= 0.25 V, VIH = 5 V and the permissible power dissipation of 0.5 mW? Assume <em>K<sub>n</sub></em> = 25 μA/V<sup>2</sup>.</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"6.2"},{"key":"B","html":"0.54"},{"key":"C","html":"4.13"},{"key":"D","html":"5.4"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(c)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/66/661d99ad8c448a8df830.png\" /></div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/04/049e389ff162c89814fc.png\" /></div>"},{"n":89,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • ELECTRONICS ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>Which of the following is/are correct statements for constant field scaling?</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img  src=\"media/69/690ac3fa2d8f0b6d4e7f.png\" /></div>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"1, 2"},{"key":"B","html":"1, 2, 3"},{"key":"C","html":"1, 2, 3, 4"},{"key":"D","html":"2, 3, 4"}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(b)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/51/51a8eccce2ca63644de5.png\" /></div>"},{"n":90,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • ELECTRONICS ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>Which of the following statements is/are correct?<br />\n1. In enhancement mode MOSFET, no extra fabrication step is required.<br />\n2. In depletion mode MOSFET, the current exists for <em>V<sub>GS</sub></em> = 0 V</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"1 only"},{"key":"B","html":"2 only"},{"key":"C","html":"1, 2"},{"key":"D","html":"Neither 1 or 2"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(c)<br />\nIn depletion MOSFET, the channel is permanently fabricated, by doping at the time of construction of MOSFET itself. Hence, an extra fabrication step is required in depletion MOSFET compared to enhancement MOSFET. Also, the current exists for <em>V<sub>GS</sub></em> = 0 V.</p>"},{"n":91,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • ELECTRONICS ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>Consider the following statements regarding Maxwell’s equations in differential form: (symbols have their usual meanings)</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img  src=\"media/b7/b727fb50e37d2996f58e.png\" /></div>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div>Of these statements:</div>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"2 and 3 are correct"},{"key":"B","html":"1 and 4 are correct"},{"key":"C","html":"1 and 3 are correct"},{"key":"D","html":"3 and 4 are correct"}],"answer":["D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"D","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(d)<br />\nStatement 4 represent the Maxwell&prime;s First Equation which is derived from the Gauss Law and<br />\nStatement 3 represent the Maxwell&prime;s Fourth Equation which is derived from Ampere Circuital Law.</p>"},{"n":92,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • ELECTRONICS ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>Given that <img align=\"absmiddle\"  src=\"media/b1/b167ba9784c1d638955d.png\" /> the total charge enclosed by the cylinder of radius 1 m with –2 ≤ z ≤ 2 m is</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"<img  src=\"media/16/16a0c9c327cac595a059.png\" />"},{"key":"B","html":"<img  src=\"media/7c/7c51efa1c03440aa627a.png\" />"},{"key":"C","html":"<img  src=\"media/67/671ce012c7167f307737.png\" />"},{"key":"D","html":"<img  src=\"media/5c/5ccf368c46a344196540.png\" />"}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(b)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/85/851103ab67fa7e3359f6.png\" /></div>"},{"n":93,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • ELECTRONICS ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>The electric field lines and equipotential surface</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"are parallel to each other."},{"key":"B","html":"are one and the same."},{"key":"C","html":"cut each other orthogonally."},{"key":"D","html":"can be inclined to each other at any angle."}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(c)<br />\nEquipotential surfaces are imaginary surfaces where the electric potential remains constant at every point. If the electric field line weren&#39;t perpendicular to the equipotential surface, it would have some component parallel to the surface. This means that moving along the surface in the direction of the parallel component would result in a change in potential. Hence, the electric field<br />\nlines are always perpendicular to an equipotential surface.</p>"},{"n":94,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • ELECTRONICS ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>A wire of diameter 1 mm and conductivity 5 × 10<sup>7</sup> S/m has 10<sup>29</sup> free electrons per cubic meter when an electric field of 10 mV/m is applied, then the current density is</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"300 kA/m<sup>2</sup>"},{"key":"B","html":"400 kA/m<sup>2</sup>"},{"key":"C","html":"500 kA/m<sup>2</sup>"},{"key":"D","html":"600 kA/m<sup>2</sup>"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(c)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/33/33ee4f60487613cf35cd.png\" /></div>"},{"n":95,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • ELECTRONICS ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>A parallel-plate capacitor with plate area of 5 cm2 and plate separation of 3 mm has a voltage 50 sin 10<sup>3</sup>t V applied to its plates. If ε = 2ε<sub>0</sub>, then the displacement current is</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"147.4 cos 10<sup>3</sup><em>t</em> nA"},{"key":"B","html":"114 cos 10<sup>3</sup><em>t</em> nA"},{"key":"C","html":"74.4 cos 10<sup>3</sup><em>t</em> nA"},{"key":"D","html":"34.4 cos 10<sup>3</sup><em>t</em> nA"}],"answer":["A"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(a)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/04/04af617b62b82d6f7213.png\" /></div>"},{"n":96,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • ELECTRONICS ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>The skin depth in a particular medium at 5 MHz is 16 cm. The skin depth in the same medium at 20 MHz is</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"4 cm"},{"key":"B","html":"8 cm"},{"key":"C","html":"2 cm"},{"key":"D","html":"6 cm"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(b)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/e7/e79b309cf7006ffb5dce.png\" /></div>"},{"n":97,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • ELECTRONICS ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>The relation among primary constants of a transmission line whose attenuation constant is independent of frequency and phase constant is linearly varying with respect to frequency is given by</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"<img  src=\"media/76/76f0b782b74e9d2e144f.png\" />"},{"key":"B","html":"<img  src=\"media/00/00706abc9d093c0e7ae2.png\" />"},{"key":"C","html":"<em>RC</em> = <em>LG</em>"},{"key":"D","html":"None of these"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(c)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/c7/c7f815920255ae41d5ca.png\" /></div>"},{"n":98,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • ELECTRONICS ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>In a pulsed radar system, the average power is 500 W and the peak power is 105 W. The duty cycle of the radar system is given by</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"0.05%"},{"key":"B","html":"0.5%"},{"key":"C","html":"0.25%"},{"key":"D","html":"0.005%"}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(b)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/34/345ad1644fc016ac74bc.png\" /></div>"},{"n":99,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • ELECTRONICS ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>An MTI radar operating at 5 GHz has PRF of 800 pps. The lowest blind speed is</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"48 m/sec"},{"key":"B","html":"96 m/sec"},{"key":"C","html":"192 m/sec"},{"key":"D","html":"24 m/sec"}],"answer":["D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"D","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(d)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/ef/ef63f8e14cebeebf7371.png\" /></div>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>"},{"n":100,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • ELECTRONICS ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>The crystal structure of an element is face centered cubic with cube side length ‘<em>a</em>’. The atomic packing fraction and radius of atom in terms of cube side will be respectively</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"<img  src=\"media/cd/cdedc2d7184b037dc904.png\" />"},{"key":"B","html":"<img  src=\"media/6a/6a3b9f445c274276ae99.png\" />"},{"key":"C","html":"<img  src=\"media/de/dea293b8932cc322b649.png\" />"},{"key":"D","html":"<img  src=\"media/86/861a48457b34801beb71.png\" />"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(c)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/0e/0e686a48052dd273783b.png\" /></div>"},{"n":101,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • ELECTRONICS ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>A certain region has magnetic permeability, μ = 5 μ<sub>0</sub> where μ<sub>0</sub> is permeability of space and magnetic flux density<img align=\"absmiddle\"  src=\"media/30/3068b0ba558249edac23.png\" /> then the value of magnetization in region will be</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"<img  src=\"media/80/80ea4671aafe40889ced.png\" />"},{"key":"B","html":"<img  src=\"media/8c/8caf5e43a4b0042d0d89.png\" />"},{"key":"C","html":"<img  src=\"media/05/050da15c95bebc32867d.png\" />"},{"key":"D","html":"<img  src=\"media/d7/d7f8ba4dcd10661d9685.png\" />"}],"answer":["A"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(a)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/9f/9f7056483cd6ac542ffd.png\" /></div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/13/133a2a492c8973e4c85e.png\" /></div>"},{"n":102,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • ELECTRONICS ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>Which of the given below statements are correctly stated regarding power loss occurring in dielectric?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"Power loss in dielectric is inversely proportional to frequency."},{"key":"B","html":"Power loss in dielectric is proportional to square of volume of dielectric used."},{"key":"C","html":"Power loss in dielectric is proportional to square of loss tangent i.e. (tan δ)<sup>2</sup>."},{"key":"D","html":"Power loss in dielectric is proportional to square of applied electric field."}],"answer":["D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"D","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(d)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/ab/ab37beec45b66d86e63e.png\" /></div>"},{"n":103,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • ELECTRONICS ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>Which of the given below relation correctly relates Hall angle ‘θ<em><sub>H</sub></em>’ to mobility in semiconductor material and magnetic flux density?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"<img  src=\"media/36/3649aedd99f7503eb1c5.png\" />"},{"key":"B","html":"<img  src=\"media/67/67f571b73ab843fe99ff.png\" />"},{"key":"C","html":"<img  src=\"media/e7/e7a5875bab36fccd31fa.png\" />"},{"key":"D","html":"<img  src=\"media/1d/1d4e43df97f474db1530.png\" />"}],"answer":["A"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(a)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/30/3055231914691db0bec1.png\" /></div>"},{"n":104,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • ELECTRONICS ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>Consider the following statements regarding phenomena of polarization in solid dielectrics:<br />\n1. Clausius Mossotti equation relates electronic polarization with relative permittivity.<br />\n2. Only ionic polarization can occur in dielectric solids consisting of alkali halides.<br />\n3. Orientation polarization does not occur in nitrobenzene before melting point as dipoles are rigidly fixed in opposite direction.<br />\nWhich of the above statement(s) is/are correct?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"2 only"},{"key":"B","html":"3 only"},{"key":"C","html":"1 and 3 only"},{"key":"D","html":"1 and 2 only"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(c)<br />\nIonic and electronic polarization both are possible in ionic solid dielectrics.</p>"},{"n":105,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • ELECTRONICS ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>Consider the following statements regarding superconductivity in materials:<br />\n1. In super conducting state, a material exhibits highest value of entropy.<br />\n2. Meissner effect is due to zero resistance state in superconductor.<br />\n3. In super conducting state, the magnetic susceptibility of material is a negative value.<br />\nWhich of the above statement(s) is/are not correct?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"1 only"},{"key":"B","html":"3 only"},{"key":"C","html":"1 and 2 only"},{"key":"D","html":"1 and 3 only"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(c)<br />\n&bull; Entropy of material at superconducting state is minimum.<br />\n&bull; The Meissner effect is the expulsion of magnetic flux from the interior of a superconductor as<br />\nit transitions to the superconducting state. It results from the perfect diamagnetism exhibited by superconductors. Perfect diamagnetism (<em>X<sub>m</sub></em>) and zero resistivity are two independent properties of super conducting state. Hence, Meissner effect is not due to zero resistance state in superconductor.</p>"},{"n":106,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • ELECTRONICS ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>The super conducting transition temperature of an element is <em>T<sub>C</sub></em> K. If the critical field at 5 K and 0 K are 9 × 10<sup>3</sup> A/m and 25 × 10<sup>3</sup> A/m respectively, the value of <em>T<sub>C</sub></em> (in K) will be</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"5.00"},{"key":"B","html":"6.25"},{"key":"C","html":"3.00"},{"key":"D","html":"4.50"}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(b)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/94/94fde60fc225b812a40b.png\" /></div>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>"},{"n":107,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • ELECTRONICS ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>Which of the given below statements are not the characteristics of ferrites?<br />\n1. Ferrites have high dielectric loss.<br />\n2. Ferrites have high resistivity.<br />\n3. Ferrites have high saturation magnetization compared to ferromagnetic materials.</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"2 and 3 only"},{"key":"B","html":"1 only"},{"key":"C","html":"1 and 3 only"},{"key":"D","html":"2 only"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(c)<br />\nFerrites have low dielectric loss making them useful in high-frequency applications and low saturation magnetization compared to ferromagnetic materials.</p>"},{"n":108,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • ELECTRONICS ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>Consider the following statements regarding carbon nano-tubes:<br />\n1. Carbon nano-tubes have low tensile strength owing to one double and two single bonds.<br />\n2. Nature of Carbon nano-tubes can be insulating, semiconducting or conducting depending on chirality of nano-tubes.<br />\n3. Carbon nano-tubes, also known as bucky tubes are represented by symbol ‘<em>C</em><sub>60</sub>’.<br />\nWhich of the above statements are not correct?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"1 and 2 only"},{"key":"B","html":"2 and 3 only"},{"key":"C","html":"1 and 3 only"},{"key":"D","html":"1, 2 and 3"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(c)<br />\nCarbon nano-tubes have high tensile strength which results from the covalent <em>sp</em><sup>2</sup> bonds formed<br />\nbetween the individual carbon atoms (i.e. one double and two single bonds). <em>C</em><sub>60</sub> is representation<br />\nof Buckminsterfullerene, also called bucky ball, which is spherical in shape.</p>"},{"n":109,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • ELECTRONICS ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>CRT has an anode voltage of 2000 V and parallel deflecting plates of length 1 cm which are 5 mm apart. The screen is 50 cm from the center of the plates. The input to deflecting plates is supplied through amplifier with overall gain of 50. If a deflection of 2.5 cm on screen is observed, then applied input voltage is</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"100 V"},{"key":"B","html":"2 V"},{"key":"C","html":"50 V"},{"key":"D","html":"1 V"}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(b)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/85/85a8f7baae0733d9e1de.png\" /></div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/c1/c1849df3dc757f9ca181.png\" /></div>"},{"n":110,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • ELECTRONICS ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>An ammeter that has an internal resistance of 100 Ω is used to measure the current through resistor <em>R</em><sub>3</sub> as shown in figures below. The percentage error caused due to meter loading is</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img  src=\"media/df/df7c0e17d71d88935836.png\" /></div>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"93.8%"},{"key":"B","html":"6.25%"},{"key":"C","html":"14.8%"},{"key":"D","html":"16.4%"}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(b)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/53/538e5ab470533e10a3d3.png\" /></div>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>"},{"n":111,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • ELECTRONICS ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>An ac bridge has the following constants.<br />\nArm <em>AB</em> - Capacitor of 0.5 μF in parallel with 1 kΩ resistance.<br />\nArm <em>AD</em> - Resistance of 2 kΩ.<br />\nArm <em>BC</em> - Capacitor of 0.5 μF<br />\nArm <em>CD</em> - Unknown capacitor for <img align=\"absmiddle\"  src=\"media/8d/8d5e06de3a4ce13dfd40.png\" /> in series.<br />\nFrequency - 1 kHz<br />\nThe dissipation factor is</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img  src=\"media/df/df78a711f03f89883591.png\" /></div>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"3.14"},{"key":"B","html":"1.05"},{"key":"C","html":"6.28"},{"key":"D","html":"0.57"}],"answer":["A"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(a)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/7b/7b3cbbce06313cac64cc.png\" /></div>"},{"n":112,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • ELECTRONICS ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>A displacement transducer with a shaft stroke of 3.0 inches, is applied to the circuit given below.<br />\nThe total resistance of the potentiometer is 5 kΩ. The applied voltage <em>V<sub>t</sub></em> is 5 V. When the wiper is 0.9 inches from B. The value of output voltage is</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img  src=\"media/d7/d7a7cfa17facfdafa9f8.png\" /></div>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"1.5 V"},{"key":"B","html":"3.0 V"},{"key":"C","html":"0.5 V"},{"key":"D","html":"2.0 V"}],"answer":["A"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(a)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/7e/7ebaac0fb59a81fe8456.png\" /></div>"},{"n":113,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • ELECTRONICS ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>The meter constant of a single phase 230 V, induction energy meter is 800 rev/kWh. The speed of rotation of meter disc for a current of 20 A at 0.7 power factor lagging will be</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"21.42 rpm"},{"key":"B","html":"42.93 rpm"},{"key":"C","html":"35.68 rpm"},{"key":"D","html":"49.73 rpm"}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(b)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/6e/6e66fa8e6a8970c9c199.png\" /></div>"},{"n":114,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • ELECTRONICS ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>Consider the following statements regarding PMMC instruments:<br />\n1. The scale is uniformly divided.<br />\n2. Power consumption is low in range of 25 μW to 200 μW.<br />\n3. The torque-weight ratio is low, hence these are less accurate.<br />\n4. Error due to stray magnetic fields are more.<br />\nWhich of the above statements are not correct?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"1 and 2 only"},{"key":"B","html":"3 and 4 only"},{"key":"C","html":"1, 2 and 3 only"},{"key":"D","html":"2, 3 and 4 only"}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(b)<br />\n&bull; The torque-weight ratio is high which gives a high accuracy.<br />\n&bull; As the operating forces are large due to strong operating flux density the errors due to stray magnetic fields are small.</p>"},{"n":115,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • ELECTRONICS ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>Consider the following statements regarding electrodynamo meter type instruments:<br />\n1. Fixed coils are wound with heavy wire carrying the main current in ammeters and wattmeters.<br />\n2. The moving coil is wound on a metallic former.<br />\n3. Air friction damping is provided for these instruments and is provided by a pair of aluminium vanes.<br />\nWhich of the above statements is/are correct?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"1 and 3 only"},{"key":"B","html":"1 only"},{"key":"C","html":"2 and 3 only"},{"key":"D","html":"1, 2 and 3"}],"answer":["A"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(a)<br />\nThe moving coil is wound either as a self-sustaining coil or else on a non-metallic former. A<br />\nmetallic former cannot be used as eddy currents would be induced in it by the alternating field.</p>"},{"n":116,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • ELECTRONICS ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>Consider the following statements regarding creeping phenomenon in measuring instruments:<br />\n1. The major cause for creeping is over compensation of friction.<br />\n2. Creeping can also be caused by excessive voltage across the potential coil, vibrations and stray magnetic fields.<br />\n3. To prevent creeping a small piece of iron is attached to the edge of the disc.<br />\nWhich of the above statements are correct?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"1 and 2 only"},{"key":"B","html":"2 and 3 only"},{"key":"C","html":"1 and 3 only"},{"key":"D","html":"1, 2 and 3"}],"answer":["D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"D","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(d)<br />\nAll statements are correct.</p>"},{"n":117,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • ELECTRONICS ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>Which of the following quantities can not be directly measured using CRO?</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img  src=\"media/5a/5a8e51be9e3f4bc55c2f.png\" /></div>\n\n<div>Select the correct answer using the codes given below.</div>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"1 and 4 only"},{"key":"B","html":"2 only"},{"key":"C","html":"3 only"},{"key":"D","html":"1, 3 and 4 only"}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(b)<br />\nThe value of current is measured indirectly by measuring voltage drop across known impedance.</p>"},{"n":118,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • ELECTRONICS ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>Consider the following statements regarding Anderson’s bridge:<br />\n1. It is the modification of the Maxwell’s inductance-capacitance bridge.<br />\n2. For measuring the low <em>Q</em> of coils, it is superior to the Maxwell’s bridge.<br />\n3. It is simple in construction compared to Maxwell’s bridge.<br />\nWhich of the above statements are correct?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"1, 2 and 3"},{"key":"B","html":"1 and 2 only"},{"key":"C","html":"2 and 3 only"},{"key":"D","html":"1 and 3 only"}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(b)<br />\nIt is the disadvantage of Anderson&rsquo;s bridge that it is more complex than its prototype Maxwell&rsquo;s bridge.</p>"},{"n":119,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • ELECTRONICS ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>Which one of the following is not a logical instruction in 8085?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"RLC"},{"key":"B","html":"DAA"},{"key":"C","html":"STC"},{"key":"D","html":"CMC"}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(b)<br />\nRLC - Rotate Accumulator Left<br />\nSTC - Set carry<br />\nCMC - Complement carry<br />\nAll the above instructions are logical instruction.<br />\nDAA - Decimal Adjust Accumulator. It is not a logical instruction</p>"},{"n":120,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • ELECTRONICS ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>In the implicit addressing mode, the address of the operand is given in the</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"Memory"},{"key":"B","html":"Register"},{"key":"C","html":"Instruction"},{"key":"D","html":"None of these"}],"answer":["D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"D","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(d)<br />\nSome instructions do not require address of the operand as they directly operate on the content of the accumulator.<br />\nEx RLC, CMA</p>"},{"n":121,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • ELECTRONICS ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>Consider the following sequence of instructions.</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img  src=\"media/83/83cfeb2a59727fd76e6f.png\" /></div>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div>What are the contents of zero flag (Z) and carry flag (CY) after the execution of instructions?</div>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"Z = 0, <em>CY</em> = 1"},{"key":"B","html":"Z = 1, <em>CY</em> = 0"},{"key":"C","html":"Z = 1, <em>CY</em> = 1"},{"key":"D","html":"Z = 1, <em>CY</em> = Unknown"}],"answer":["D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"D","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(d)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/b5/b515c8e2af4b6a8d99a7.png\" /></div>"},{"n":122,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • ELECTRONICS ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>Execution of RSTn instruction causes the stack pointer be</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"increment by two"},{"key":"B","html":"decrement by two"},{"key":"C","html":"remain unaffected"},{"key":"D","html":"none of these"}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(b)<br />\nRST instruction is equivalent to a one-byte call instruction.</p>"},{"n":123,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • ELECTRONICS ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>Consider the following instructions.<br />\n1. PUSH PSW<br />\n2. PUSH A<br />\n3. POP A<br />\nWhich of the following instruction is used to save accumulator contents on the stack?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"1 and 2 only"},{"key":"B","html":"1 only"},{"key":"C","html":"2 only"},{"key":"D","html":"3 only"}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(b)<br />\nFormat of PUSH instruction is PUSH register pair/PSW.<br />\nPUSH PSW copy content of Accumulator and flag on to stack.</p>"},{"n":124,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • ELECTRONICS ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>Which one of the following statement corresponding to execution of RIM instructions is not correct?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"It checks the status of all the interrupt."},{"key":"B","html":"Content of SID pin is copied to MSB of accumulator."},{"key":"C","html":"It is used to check the pending interrupt."},{"key":"D","html":"It can handle interrupts and serial I/O."}],"answer":["A"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(a)<br />\nIt does not provide the status of the TRAP and INTR interrupt.</p>"},{"n":125,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • ELECTRONICS ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>Consider the addressing modes representation in 8086 microprocessor:<br />\n(i) ADD AL, [DI + 16] : Index addressing mode<br />\n(ii) MOV AX, [AX + DI]: Based-index addressing mode<br />\n(iii) ADD CX, [BX + SI + 16]: Based addressing mode<br />\nWhich of the above are correctly represented?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"(i) and (ii) only"},{"key":"B","html":"(ii) and (iii) only"},{"key":"C","html":"(i) and (iii) only"},{"key":"D","html":"All (i), (ii) and (iii)"}],"answer":["A"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(a)<br />\nADD CX, [BX + SI + 16]: Based index with displacement mode.</p>"},{"n":126,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • ELECTRONICS ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>In which mode, the CPU periodically reads an internal flag of 8279 to check whether any key is pressed or not with key pressure?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"Interrupt mode"},{"key":"B","html":"Polled mode"},{"key":"C","html":"Decoded mode"},{"key":"D","html":"Encoded mode"}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(b)<br />\nIn the polled mode, the CPU periodically reads an internal flag of 8279 to check whether any key is pressed or not with key pressure.</p>"},{"n":127,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • ELECTRONICS ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>Which of the following feature is not true for 8237?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"Each channel can be programmed independently."},{"key":"B","html":"Each channel has 16-bit address and 14-bit counter."},{"key":"C","html":"Each channel can transfer data upto 64 kb."},{"key":"D","html":"8237 has three channels which can be used over three I/O devices."}],"answer":["D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"D","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(d)<br />\n8237 has four channels which can be used over four I/O devices.</p>"},{"n":128,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • ELECTRONICS ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>An optical fiber in air has numerical aperture of 0.25. The acceptance angle for skew rays which change direction by 120° at each reflection is</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"38.5°"},{"key":"B","html":"23.6°"},{"key":"C","html":"14.47°"},{"key":"D","html":"30°"}],"answer":["D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"D","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(d)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/78/78164b9b690699359fcd.png\" /></div>"},{"n":129,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • ELECTRONICS ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>The velocity of a satellite in a circular orbit having height of 19200 km is</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"3.953 km/sec"},{"key":"B","html":"4.323 km/sec"},{"key":"C","html":"3 km/sec"},{"key":"D","html":"4 km/sec"}],"answer":["A"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(a)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/c3/c3dee6c841bf4cb53005.png\" /></div>"},{"n":130,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • ELECTRONICS ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>A satellite is in elliptical orbit with perigee height of 1100 km and apogee height of 4100 km. If the mean earth radius is 6400 km, the length of major axis is</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"8900 km"},{"key":"B","html":"9000 km"},{"key":"C","html":"8700 km"},{"key":"D","html":"18000 km"}],"answer":["D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"D","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(d)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/77/77c01bffda8f8ae349d6.png\" /></div>"},{"n":131,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • ELECTRONICS ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>The downlink transmission rate in a satellite link is 40 Mbps and the required [<em>E<sub>b</sub></em>/<em>N</em><sub>0</sub>] at the ground station receiver is 9 dB. The required <img align=\"absmiddle\"  src=\"media/38/3809a008afa44e0faeca.png\" /> of the link is</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"67 dB-Hz"},{"key":"B","html":"76 dB-Hz"},{"key":"C","html":"85 dB-Hz"},{"key":"D","html":"100 dB-Hz"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(c)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/d2/d2f197db75e83e11caea.png\" /></div>"},{"n":132,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • ELECTRONICS ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>A satellite transponder has a bandwidth of 36 MHz. Earth stations use RRC filters with α = 0.5.<br />\nThe maximum bit rate that can be sent through this transponder with BPSK is</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"24 Mbps"},{"key":"B","html":"48 Mbps"},{"key":"C","html":"12 Mbps"},{"key":"D","html":"36 Mbps"}],"answer":["A"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(a)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/c1/c1df790e5f747d5aba14.png\" /></div>"},{"n":133,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • ELECTRONICS ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>Consider the following statements regarding satellite communication system:<br />\n1. At the transponder, there is RF to RF conversion whereas at receiving earth station RF to IF conversion takes place.<br />\n2. At the transponder, there is IF to RF conversion whereas at transmitting earth station IF to RF conversion takes place.<br />\n3. At the transponder, horn antenna is used for receiving and transmitting the signals.<br />\n4. The downlink frequency is more than the uplink frequency.<br />\nThe correct statements is/are</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"1 and 2 only"},{"key":"B","html":"2 and 3 only"},{"key":"C","html":"3 and 4 only"},{"key":"D","html":"1 and 3 only"}],"answer":["D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"D","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(d)<br />\nuplink frequency &gt; downlink frequency<br />\nAt the transponder, RF to RF conversion takes place.</p>"},{"n":134,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • ELECTRONICS ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>Which of the following resources can cause deadlock?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"Files"},{"key":"B","html":"Printers"},{"key":"C","html":"Shared programs"},{"key":"D","html":"All of the above"}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(b)<br />\nFiles and programs can be shared but printer cannot be shared.</p>"},{"n":135,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • ELECTRONICS ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>Consider a relational table <em>R</em> that is in 3 NF but not in BCNF.<br />\nThe correct statement is</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"<em>R</em> has a non-trivial functional dependency <em>X</em> → <em>A</em> where <em>X</em> is not a superkey and <em>A</em> is non prime attribute and <em>X</em> is not a proper subset of any key."},{"key":"B","html":"A cell in <em>R</em> holds a set instead of an atomic value."},{"key":"C","html":"R has a trivial functional dependency <em>X</em> → <em>A</em>."},{"key":"D","html":"<em>R</em> has a non-trivial functional dependency <em>X</em> → <em>A</em> where <em>X</em> is not a superkey and <em>A</em> is prime attribute."}],"answer":["D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"D","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(d)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/5c/5c36ffe653a74b66dd7d.png\" /></div>"},{"n":136,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • ELECTRONICS ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>Consider the following statements :<br />\n1. Kernel level threads are implemented by OS.<br />\n2. Kernel level threads scheduling does not require hardware support.<br />\n3. ‘Gang’ scheduling is used in threads.<br />\nThe correct statements is/are</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"1 and 2 only"},{"key":"B","html":"2 and 3 only"},{"key":"C","html":"1 and 3 only"},{"key":"D","html":"All of the above"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(c)<br />\nKernel level threads scheduling requires hardware support.</p>"},{"n":137,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • ELECTRONICS ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p><strong>Statement (I):</strong> A job moves from ready to suspend ready if space is not available in the main memory.<br />\n<strong>Statement (II):</strong> In suspend ready state process is available in the secondary memory but not available for execution, until it is loaded in main memory.</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"a"},{"key":"B","html":"b"},{"key":"C","html":"c"},{"key":"D","html":"d"}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(b)</p>"},{"n":138,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • ELECTRONICS ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p><strong>Statement (I):</strong> Carey-Foster bridge is used for measurement of capacitance in terms of a standard mutual inductance.<br />\n<strong>Statement (II):</strong> Carey-Foster bridge can also be used for measurement of mutual inductance in terms of a standard capacitance. In this case it is known as Heydweiller bridge.</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"a"},{"key":"B","html":"b"},{"key":"C","html":"c"},{"key":"D","html":"d"}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(b)<br />\nBoth statements are correct but statement-II is not the correct explanation of statement-I.</p>"},{"n":139,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • ELECTRONICS ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p><strong>Statement (I):</strong> Greater the number of valence electrons in the outer orbit of material, more is the magnetic property of material.<br />\n<strong>Statement (II):</strong> Magnetization inside a material is mainly caused due to uncompensated electronic spin.</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"a"},{"key":"B","html":"b"},{"key":"C","html":"c"},{"key":"D","html":"d"}],"answer":["D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"D","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(d)<br />\nTwo electrons having opposite spin in a shell results in cancellation of their individual magnetic field hence showing zero magnetization.</p>"},{"n":140,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • ELECTRONICS ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p><strong>Statement (I)::</strong> The solution of Poisson’s equation is the same as the solution of Laplace equation.<br />\n<strong>Statement (II)::</strong> Laplace equation is a special case of Poisson’s equation for source free regions.</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"a"},{"key":"B","html":"b"},{"key":"C","html":"c"},{"key":"D","html":"d"}],"answer":["D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"D","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(d)<br />\nIf &rho; = 0, Poisson&rsquo;s equation reduces to Laplace equation but solution of Poisson&rsquo;s equation is not<br />\nsame as that of Laplace equation.</p>"},{"n":141,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • ELECTRONICS ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p><strong>Statement (I):</strong> Operating system controls and co-ordinates the efficient use of a hardware among the multiple user programs.<br />\n<strong>Statement (II):</strong> Efficient context switching to share resources among the user in operating system.</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"a"},{"key":"B","html":"b"},{"key":"C","html":"c"},{"key":"D","html":"d"}],"answer":["A"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(a)<br />\nIn operating system efficient context, switching to share resources among the user in operating (Fixed time quantum).</p>"},{"n":142,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • ELECTRONICS ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p><strong>Statement (I):</strong> In differential amplifier large <em>R<sub>E</sub></em> needs higher biasing voltage to set the operating Q point of the transistors.<br />\n<strong>Statement (II):</strong> Due to <em>R<sub>E</sub></em> increases the overall chip area.</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"a"},{"key":"B","html":"b"},{"key":"C","html":"c"},{"key":"D","html":"d"}],"answer":["A"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(a)</p>"},{"n":143,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • ELECTRONICS ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>Statement (I): Static power dissipation will be present when input is fixed at logic ‘0’ OR logic ‘1’ input.<br />\nStatement (II): Dynamic power dissipation will be present when input changes from ‘0’ to ‘1’ or ‘1’ to ‘0’ transition.</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"a"},{"key":"B","html":"b"},{"key":"C","html":"c"},{"key":"D","html":"d"}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(b)</p>"},{"n":144,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • ELECTRONICS ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p><strong>Statement (I):</strong> If the reverse bias is increased at the base collector junction then the base width gradually becomes narrow known as base narrowing or base width modulation.<br />\n<strong>Statement (II):</strong> BJT has two resistance, so it has more variation in voltages, so it is noisy.</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"a"},{"key":"B","html":"b"},{"key":"C","html":"c"},{"key":"D","html":"d"}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(b)</p>"},{"n":145,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • ELECTRONICS ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p><strong>Statement (I):</strong> In salint pole generator non-uniform air gap length is present between stator and rotor.<br />\n<strong>Statement (II):</strong> Due to large diameter salint pole generator cannot be operated at high speeds.</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"a"},{"key":"B","html":"b"},{"key":"C","html":"c"},{"key":"D","html":"d"}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(b)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/7e/7e335add06411f0120ea.png\" /></div>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>"},{"n":146,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • ELECTRONICS ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p><strong>Statement (I):</strong> Determinant of Augmented incidence matrix of closed loop is equal to zero.<br />\n<strong>Statement (II):</strong> Network topology is applied only for planar networks.</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"a"},{"key":"B","html":"b"},{"key":"C","html":"c"},{"key":"D","html":"d"}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(b)</p>"},{"n":147,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • ELECTRONICS ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p><strong>Statement (I):</strong> If the transfer function of individual components of a system are known, the overall transfer function can be determined by taking the product of individual transfer function. This is, of course, true if there is no loading of system by the connected devices.<br />\n<strong>Statement (II):</strong> The transfer function offers a picture of the dynamic characteristics of linear time invariant system.</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"a"},{"key":"B","html":"b"},{"key":"C","html":"c"},{"key":"D","html":"d"}],"answer":["A"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(a)</p>"},{"n":148,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • ELECTRONICS ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p><img align=\"absmiddle\"  src=\"media/ad/ad2099a49bec7c2b918e.png\" /></p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"a"},{"key":"B","html":"b"},{"key":"C","html":"c"},{"key":"D","html":"d"}],"answer":["A"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(a)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/e7/e71a1da75b97668ee039.png\" /></div>"},{"n":149,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • ELECTRONICS ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p><strong>Statement (I):</strong> If a Gaussian process <em>X</em>(<em>t</em>) is applied to a stable linear filter, then the random process Y(t) developed at the output of the filter is also Gaussian.<br />\n<strong>Statement (II):</strong> If a Gaussian process is wide sense stationary, then the process is also stationary in strict sense.</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"a"},{"key":"B","html":"b"},{"key":"C","html":"c"},{"key":"D","html":"d"}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(b)</p>"},{"n":150,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • ELECTRONICS ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p><strong>Statement (I):</strong> Case II is slower than the circuit of</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img  src=\"media/d2/d243b44bad7d7a1e1cce.png\" /></div>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><strong>Statement (II):</strong> Case II has additional capacitance on internal node which charges to <em>V<sub>DD</sub></em>-<em>V<sub>th </sub></em>which takes some of the charging current normally going in ground.</div>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"a"},{"key":"B","html":"b"},{"key":"C","html":"c"},{"key":"D","html":"d"}],"answer":["A"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(a)</p>"}],"math":false,"scoring":"marks","sections":[{"name":"Technical","start":0,"count":150}],"stats":{"questions":150,"maxMarks":300,"types":{"MCQ":150},"withoutKey":0}};
